include "../../linux.mereo"

-- REJECTED on purpose: a constant access that runs past a known backing.
--
-- Everything needed is present when the program is read. The width must be a
-- literal 1, 2, 4 or 8 by rule -- a run-time width would be a run-time load
-- size, which the machine does not have -- and a backing's size is the number
-- the emitter puts in the array's brackets. So `[block + 100 : 1]` against
-- `block is 8 bytes` is decidable, and was accepted: GCC does not warn at the
-- shipped flags either, so it reached the binary and read past the frame.
--
-- A view laid over a backing has always been fit-checked this way (`backing
-- 'block' is 4 bytes, too small to view 16`). Raw accesses simply never went
-- through it.
--
-- What is NOT checked, and cannot be: an index computed at run time. The value
-- comes from outside the program, so no amount of whole-program analysis
-- reaches it -- the answer there is a bound the compiler can prove, which is
-- what the loop idiom in docs/performance.md is about. This check only claims
-- the decidable half.
--
--   refused:  reads 101 bytes into 'block', which is 8 bytes

program goes
  block is 8 bytes

  n is [block + 100 : 1]

  ensure n >= 0
end
